I wrote two task files and the issue responses for this session. The commit didn't happen: session_plan/ is in .gitignore. The files are on disk, and the commit line in the instructions ends in || true, so it's set up to tolerate a failed commit. I didn't force-add them.

Task 1: warn when a reply is cut off at the output-token limit (session_plan/task_01.md, product)
• Today the assessment ran yoyo --max-tokens 30 -p "numbers 1 to 200" and got only 1 to 15, with exit code 0 and nothing on stderr. Claude Code fixed the same problem in 2.1.300.
• The task starts by checking how yoagent handles a stop caused by the token limit, and by listing every place in src/ that reads that stop reason.
• The fix is one warning line on stderr, in both -p and the REPL. Stdout stays exactly as streamed.
• The exit code stays 0 on purpose, so users who set a low max_tokens deliberately don't see every run fail. The commit body will say this.
• It also lists the loop's own -p calls that could hit the warning.
• Tests: the warning's wording, the stop-reason routing, and a normal-stop case that must produce no warning (today's stderr, unchanged). If the existing stub server can fake a cut-off, there's also an end-to-end test of stdout, stderr and exit code together.

Task 2: Part of #869, clearing out the oldest backlog issue I can act on (session_plan/task_02.md, product)
• #858 is older, but only a human can edit the file it's about, so I skipped it.
• The slice: after /cd, auto-approve entries that came only from the launch project's .yoyo.toml [permissions] allow stop applying. Entries from user config or --allow keep working.
• This only takes permissions away, so it doesn't need the trust gate. It also closes a real hole: an allow like cargo * from project A could auto-run cargo build in project B.
• It measures first. If the launch project's allows already stop at /cd, the deliverable is a test that pins that, plus a note on #869.
• `/permissions has to show the list as it stands after /cd`, and the task ends with a comment on #869.

